MID_EV11_005_m01: <EDH>
MID_EV11_005_m01_001: Haven't forgotten anything, have you? We've got to\nhead out while it's still early.
MID_EV11_005_m01_002: Pop, how do we even get to Dagsion?
MID_EV11_005_m01_003: To start, we'll leave the village and go south. Then\nwe head for Carmenta Road.
MID_EV11_005_m01_004: After that, we can take a carriage straight to\nDagsion, and...uh...
MID_EV11_005_m01_005: Tialla! Peter! What are you doing here?
MID_EV11_005_m01_006: You're taking us with you to Dagsion!
MID_EV11_005_m01_007: Well...if Tialla's going, I'm going.
MID_EV11_005_m01_008: Hey, slow down. We're not talking about a day trip.
MID_EV11_005_m01_009: It's fine! I already talked it over with Grandpa.
MID_EV11_005_m01_010: "Books aren't enough. See the world with your own\neyes." Grandpa's words, exactly.
MID_EV11_005_m01_011: He said, "It's a good opportunity. Get what you can\nfrom it." Then he gave me a buncha spending money.
MID_EV11_005_m01_012: And if I get the chance, I should pay my respects to\nCai's mother...right?
MID_EV11_005_m01_013: I asked my parents. Dad was against it, but...
MID_EV11_005_m01_014: Mom was into the whole idea. She kinda shoved it\nright past my dad.
MID_EV11_005_m01_015: Wow! Thanks, you two! I'm feeling a lot better about\nthis trip.
MID_EV11_005_m01_016: I dunno... This is a lot to handle.
MID_EV11_005_m01_017: But I guess if your folks are OK with it, we can make\nit work.
MID_EV11_005_m01_018: Really?! Thank you, Castor!
MID_EV11_006_m01: <EDH>
MID_EV11_006_m01_001: Looks like a dead end. Huh? What's going on there?
MID_EV11_006_m01_002: It's that bossy thief from the other day, isn't it?\nBut who's the other one?
MID_EV11_006_m01_003: Listen. That job wasn't what you said it'd be.
MID_EV11_006_m01_004: I didn't sign up to mess with a crew like that. Wasn't\nworth it for the money offered.
MID_EV11_006_m01_005: Explain to me why I don't have the jewel. Oh, yes.\nIt's because you failed, isn't it?
MID_EV11_006_m01_006: All you've given me is mistakes and excuses. There's\nno value in keeping such an incompetent oaf alive.
MID_EV11_006_m01_007: Maybe there's no value in keeping you alive!
MID_EV11_006_m01_008: Waaagh!
MID_EV11_006_m01_009: Idiot...
MID_EV11_006_m01_010: Mm?
MID_EV11_006_m01_011: She froze. Did she see us?
MID_EV11_006_m01_012: Hold on. She's dangerous. I doubt even I could\nhandle her.
MID_EV11_006_m01_013: But if it comes to it, we'll do what we have to.
MID_EV11_006_m01_014: They failed to retrieve the right eye, but it'll end up\nin Dagsion anyway, no doubt.
MID_EV11_006_m01_015: Like an insect drawn to a flame. Everything is\nproceeding according to your will, Raksha.
MID_EV11_006_m01_016: Hmph. *sigh*
MID_EV11_006_m01_017: She's gone, but she and her friends are bad news.
MID_EV11_006_m01_018: Pop, what's going on with this jewel?\nWho is Mom? Really?
MID_EV11_006_m01_019: I don't understand it myself. Best thing would be to\nask her.
MID_EV11_008_m01: <EDH>
MID_EV11_008_m01_001: Good work, gang. We made it.
MID_EV11_008_m01_015: <NCA>Look around.
MID_EV11_008_m01_002: Is this Dagsion? It's different than I imagined.
MID_EV11_008_m01_003: What we mostly have here is stables, water for\nhorses, mangers full of fodder... Hmmm...
MID_EV11_008_m01_004: You're both clueless. This is just a posthouse.
MID_EV11_008_m01_016: Like I told you before we left, we're catching a\ncarriage here.
MID_EV11_008_m01_006: Carriages are a highly convenient form of transport,\nconnecting Dagda through all its major highways.
MID_EV11_008_m01_007: They're not cheap, but anyone with enough coin can\nuse them.
MID_EV11_008_m01_008: Ever ridden in one, Tialla?
MID_EV11_008_m01_009: Ages ago, when we moved to Ribeira, I rode on one\nwith my grandpa.
MID_EV11_008_m01_010: Well, if we wait here, one ought to show up. You kids\nshould take this chance to get some rest.
MID_EV11_008_m01_011: Whoa! Nice carriage! Are we really gonna ride in it?
MID_EV11_008_m01_012: It's so big! And it has more passenger cars than I\nthought it'd have.
MID_EV11_008_m01_013: I'll pay our way. You all get on board and make\nyourselves comfy, OK?
MID_EV11_008_m01_014: Yes, sir!
MID_EV11_100_m01: <EDH>
MID_EV11_100_m01_001: The truth is I've got a death sentence on me, from\nwhen I used to live in Dagsion.
MID_EV11_100_m01_002: On your 15th birthday, my reprieve is over.
MID_EV11_100_m01_003: Well, there you have it, my reason for heading\nto Dagsion.
MID_EV11_100_m01_004: Why am I just hearing about all this now?
MID_EV11_100_m01_005: I... I had planned to go alone.
MID_EV11_100_m01_006: But your hand... What happened to you isn't normal.\nIt's not something I can deal with on my own.
MID_EV11_100_m01_007: We need your mother to take a look at this. So I\ndecided to bring you along.
MID_EV11_100_m01_008: You do want to meet her, don't you?
MID_EV11_100_m01_009: Honestly, I'm not sure. It just seems so normal to me\nto not have a mom.
MID_EV11_100_m01_010: Yeah, you've never known her.
MID_EV11_100_m01_011: And now I worry that we should have left it that\nway, kept you in your familiar life back in the village.
MID_EV11_100_m01_012: Is all of this the reason you raised me the way\nyou did?
MID_EV11_100_m01_013: I wanted you to have a proper life even after I\nwas gone.
MID_EV11_100_m01_014: You've still got a ways to go, but I hammered all my\nskills into you.
MID_EV11_100_m01_015: And you should be grateful! I used to be kinda\nfamous in the old days, you know?
MID_EV11_100_m01_016: Pop... That death sentence... Can't you just\nrun away?
MID_EV11_100_m01_017: Not possible. The man who gave you the message—\nhe's tracking us, not too far behind.
MID_EV11_100_m01_018: Even among the temple vulkans, he's one of the best.
MID_EV11_100_m01_019: <NCA>Ask about the vulkans.
MID_EV11_100_m01_020: <NCB>Ask about the man with the message.
MID_EV11_100_m01_021: <ACA>What are vulkans anyway, Pop?
MID_EV11_100_m01_022: <ACA>They're also known as the celestial knights because\nthey're elite units under divine command.
MID_EV11_100_m01_023: <ACA>Celestial... You mean like gods.
MID_EV11_100_m01_024: <ACA>The vulkans are a select bunch, even among\nthe priests.
MID_EV11_100_m01_025: <ACA>They're different than regular priests because they're\ngranted their own military standing and authority.
MID_EV11_100_m01_026: <ACB>So that messenger, was he someone you knew?
MID_EV11_100_m01_027: <ACB>Yeah. He was a good friend at one time.
MID_EV11_100_m01_028: <ACB>He's dead serious and focused. He's the very best,\nand with him tracking us, there's no running away.
MID_EV11_100_m01_029: <ACB>He was your friend, but now he's watching you so\nyou don't escape?
MID_EV11_100_m01_030: <ACB>Grown-ups have to deal with a lot of challenging\nsituations, and—
MID_EV11_100_m01_031: Pop, aren't you afraid to die?
MID_EV11_100_m01_032: Most of us fear death, Cai. Of course I'm afraid.
MID_EV11_100_m01_033: But I've lived a good life. I've got no regrets.
MID_EV11_100_m01_034: Your mother was such an incredible woman.
MID_EV11_100_m01_035: And best of all, you grew up right. I'm very proud\nof you.
MID_EV11_101_m01: <EDH>
MID_EV11_101_m01_001: Cai, may I speak with you?
MID_EV11_101_m01_002: Tialla? Of course. But why so formal?
MID_EV11_101_m01_003: I want you to have this. It's a little late, but it's your\nbirthday present.
MID_EV11_101_m01_004: A pocket knife! I love it!
MID_EV11_101_m01_005: My old one broke a while ago.
MID_EV11_101_m01_006: I bought it from a peddler who came by the village\nthe other day.
MID_EV11_101_m01_007: I fastened the leather cord around the hilt myself.\nOr I tried to, at least.
MID_EV11_101_m01_008: The day before your birthday, I said I had to run an\nerrand for Grandpa. I was fibbing.
MID_EV11_101_m01_009: It's hard to wrap cord around a hilt, but I wanted to\nget it right. I just finished a little while ago.
MID_EV11_101_m01_010: Thanks, Tialla! I'll take good care of it.
MID_EV11_101_m01_011: Haha... I'm glad that you like it.
MID_EV11_101_m01_012: You're kind of...different, aren't you, Cai?
MID_EV11_101_m01_013: You're a country kid, but you have this sort of...\ngrace in how you do things.
MID_EV11_101_m01_014: I don't know. Like you're...polished, maybe? Like\nyou grew up in a city.
MID_EV11_101_m01_015: You think so? I was born and raised in Ribeira.\nUnlike you, I'm a true-blue country bumpkin.
MID_EV11_101_m01_016: And you know what Pop is like. Not even a hint of\ncity class.
MID_EV11_101_m01_017: Well, maybe your mother was more of a city person.
MID_EV11_101_m01_018: I guess she's in Dagsion, so you might be right.
MID_EV11_101_m01_019: I really am looking forward to meeting her, Cai.
MID_EV11_101_m01_020: I mean, if it weren't for your mother, you wouldn't\nbe here, right?
MID_EV11_101_m01_021: What kind of person is she? I can't wait to find out.
MID_EV11_101_m01_022: To be honest, I'm nervous about meeting her.
MID_EV11_101_m01_023: At first, I thought maybe she wouldn't want to\nsee me.
MID_EV11_101_m01_024: Why would you think that?
MID_EV11_101_m01_025: Well...because... Why haven't I met her before?
MID_EV11_101_m01_026: I can't help but wonder why she's stayed away.
MID_EV11_101_m01_027: But if she's still alive, and you can go and see her,\nyou really ought to, Cai.
MID_EV11_101_m01_028: I'm sorry, Tialla. Here I am talking about my family,\nand I wasn't even thinking about yours.
MID_EV11_010_m01: <EDH>
MID_EV11_010_m01_001: Ugh... I'm glad we get to ride in this carriage and\ndon't have to walk the whole way.
MID_EV11_010_m01_002: But sitting here so long is making my backside numb.\nIt was nice at first, but I can't stand it much longer.
MID_EV11_010_m01_003: It's all the cobblestones on the road. The vibrations\nput my butt to sleep!
MID_EV11_010_m01_004: Speaking of "numb," you seem unfazed by\nall this, Cai.
MID_EV11_010_m01_018: <NCA>Say you're used to it.
MID_EV11_010_m01_019: <NCB>Say it doesn't bother you.
MID_EV11_010_m01_005: <ACA>It was hard for me at first, but I'm used to it now.
MID_EV11_010_m01_006: <ACA>I think all this jostling is making me nauseous. I wish\nwe'd hurry up and get there.
MID_EV11_010_m01_020: <ACB>I'm fine, and you can't beat the view.
MID_EV11_010_m01_021: <ACB>Really? It all looks the same to me. Don't you think\nit's boring?
MID_EV11_010_m01_007: Tialla, what's the big thing that looks like a bridge?
MID_EV11_010_m01_008: That's an aqueduct. It carries water to the city. The\nwater comes from snow melting in the mountains.
MID_EV11_010_m01_009: That means Dagsion is at the end of the aqueduct.
MID_EV11_010_m01_010: It should be coming into view... Kids, look outside,\nup the road.
MID_EV11_010_m01_011: Just ahead, that big stretch of water... Is that\nthe ocean?
MID_EV11_010_m01_017: Looks like the aqueduct goes to that big island in the\nmiddle of it.
MID_EV11_010_m01_012: Oh, wow! The island's packed with buildings.
MID_EV11_010_m01_013: Hey! What else are you seeing? Tell me, Peter!
MID_EV11_010_m01_014: It's like... It's like the whole island is one big city.\nIt's amazing.
MID_EV11_010_m01_015: Here at last. The very heart of the Dagdan Empire.
MID_EV11_010_m01_016: The imperial capital, Dagsion—biggest city, biggest\npopulation on the whole continent.
MID_NR11_00_m01: <SH>
MID_NR11_00_m01_001: Dagsion, imperial capital.
MID_NR11_00_m01_002: This vast city covers an island that dominates Lake\nBaal, a freshwater lake in Northern Dagda.
MID_NR11_00_m01_003: The imperial capital, with its bridges and aqueducts\nthat span the lake, has come to look like a giant ship\ntethered in the water, crowded with passengers.
MID_NR11_00_m01_004: Over a million souls call this place home. It is the\nbeating heart of all Dagda. The empire has long been\ncentered here, its history alive and well.
MID_NR11_00_m01_005: The city is known for its three layers, each highly\ndistinct from the others.
MID_NR11_00_m01_006: The port is in lower Dagsion, where Lake Baal's\nbounty is brought in off the waters. Most citizens\nlive nearby in cramped neighborhoods.
MID_NR11_00_m01_007: In the middle layer is the colosseum—one of the\ngreat drivers of commerce—as are the city's \nbustling markets and inns.
MID_NR11_00_m01_008: Upper Dagsion is a realm of refined pleasures,\nsuch as the theater and bathhouse, and is also\nwhere visiting dignitaries stay.
MID_NR11_00_m01_009: Most of all, it's where the Divine Emperor long ago\nplaced the gods and their temples, where they have\nsince ruled—religion and politics wed as one.
MID_NR11_00_m01_010: The imperial city of Dagsion has this in common \nwith our Garreg Mach Monastery—it soars ever\nupward on its slopes, a truly majestic sight.
MID_NR11_00_m01_011: —Saint Cichol, "Dagdan Travels,"\nPart 3, Chapter 2
MID_EV11_102_m01: <EDH>
MID_EV11_102_m01_001: Castor, it's good you made it back here.
MID_EV11_102_m01_002: When was the last time we spoke face-to-face\nlike this?
MID_EV11_102_m01_003: It's been 16 years since Windstorm and Corundum\nwere a team. Can you believe it?
MID_EV11_102_m01_004: Anyway, I'm sure I've caused you a lot of trouble\nsince then.
MID_EV11_102_m01_005: Just a glance at her face can...stir up pangs of\nguilt in me.
MID_EV11_102_m01_006: Pangs like... Well, maybe I should have looked the\nother way that time in Yaaman village.
MID_EV11_102_m01_007: Hey, that's not the Corundum Centurio I know...the\nman who always does his loyal duty, whatever it is?
MID_EV11_102_m01_008: If we took the duty-bound soldier out of you, there\nwouldn't be anything left, would there?
MID_EV11_102_m01_009: If I'm being honest, I might have thought the Divine\nSovereign's judgment was a bit...harsh.
MID_EV11_102_m01_010: You can say that, but if you follow the letter of the\nlaw, there's no other way.
MID_EV11_102_m01_011: Kidnapping a god, no matter the reason, merits\ndeath. That's just how it is.
MID_EV11_102_m01_012: The Sovereign can't bend the rules. Every rank has\nits duty.
MID_EV11_102_m01_013: But when I think about the circumstances... Let's just\nsay I can't blame you.
MID_EV11_102_m01_014: Doesn't matter now. Don't want you feeling bound by\nyour guilt.
MID_EV11_102_m01_015: *cough* *cough* *cough*
MID_EV11_102_m01_016: Castor... What's wrong with you? Did you...\nYou aren't...
MID_EV11_102_m01_017: Yeah...
MID_EV11_102_m01_018: It's pretty serious. I've had it for, oh, five years now.
MID_EV11_102_m01_019: Not much hope for a cure. I'm living on borrowed\ntime no matter what.
MID_EV11_102_m01_020: Listen... My boy, Cai? Don't tell him...ever.
MID_EV11_102_m01_021: But... Alright.
MID_EV11_102_m01_022: I'm not real scared of being put to death. I'm almost\ngrateful to the Sovereign for it.
MID_EV11_102_m01_023: I got the time to watch over Cai, make sure he grew\nup...just right.
MID_EV11_102_m01_024: How does you-know-who feel about it?
MID_EV11_102_m01_025: I'll have to see her and find out. I've got time for that,\ndon't I?
MID_EV11_102_m01_026: I have no say in the matter, so long as it's done by\ntomorrow morning.
MID_EV11_102_m01_027: Good man. In that case, I guess I better put my\nthoughts in order.
MID_EV11_102_m01_028: While we're on the topic of women, what's that\nsweet scent I detect on you, Centurio?
MID_EV11_102_m01_029: Never known you to get in those kinds of\nentanglements. You married or somethin'?
MID_EV11_102_m01_030: What?! N-no! Definitely... No! Nothing of the sort!
MID_EV11_102_m01_031: I'm a servant of Aurora. I couldn't fulfill my duties if\nI had any familial attachments.
MID_EV11_102_m01_032: Hehe. Sure, sure. Sounds like something's going\non. There's more to you than I realized.
MID_EV11_102_m01_033: Listen, Centurio. Having a family... It's better than\nyou think.
MID_EV11_013_m01: <EDH>
MID_EV11_013_m01_001: I'm heading in. It's so dark.
MID_EV11_013_m01_002: What was that?! Someone's attacking?!
MID_EV11_013_m01_003: And who is entering my chambers from such an\nunusual place?
MID_EV11_013_m01_004: Hello. It's been a while. Glad to see you in such\ngood spirits.
MID_EV11_013_m01_005: Is it really... Castor?! You've come to see me!
MID_EV11_013_m01_006: What in the world? I'd never have guessed the\nsewers led here.
MID_EV11_013_m01_007: Oh! I'm not seeing this. Nope.
MID_EV11_013_m01_008: Centurio! Shoulda known you'd tag along.
MID_EV11_013_m01_009: Oh, this takes me back, seeing you. You look less...\nyoung than I remember.
MID_EV11_013_m01_010: But you have the same air about you. I'm so happy to\nsee you again.
MID_EV11_013_m01_011: But if you returned to Dagsion, I suppose that\nmust mean...
MID_EV11_013_m01_012: Yeah...tomorrow. I'll turn myself over to the temples.\nNo doubt they'll execute me right away.
MID_EV11_013_m01_013: That's absurd. Don't do this. Centurio, I order you\nto make him desist!
MID_EV11_013_m01_014: This is Lord Solel's decree. It's beyond someone of\nmy standing to interfere.
MID_EV11_013_m01_015: I was always going to leave this world ahead of you.\nI'll just be leaving a little sooner now.
MID_EV11_013_m01_016: You just returned to me, and now you intend to\nabandon me again?!
MID_EV11_013_m01_017: How do you think I've felt all this time?
MID_EV11_013_m01_018: I... I'm sorry.
MID_EV11_013_m01_050: <NCA>Ask what's going on.
MID_EV11_013_m01_019: Pop, what's all this about?
MID_EV11_013_m01_021: I'm a little slow on the introductions, Cai.\nThis...is your mother.
MID_EV11_013_m01_022: Also known as Aurora, Navigator's Clarion.
MID_EV11_013_m01_023: What? Aurora, like the statue we had at home?
MID_EV11_013_m01_024: A real goddess! And she's Cai's mother?
MID_EV11_013_m01_025: What? Cai? I hadn't mentally prepared myself for\nthis. Well, how could I?
MID_EV11_013_m01_028: It's so nice to meet Cai's distinguished mother!\nI'm Tialla, Cai's friend.
MID_EV11_013_m01_029: Umm... I'm Peter. Cai and I have been friends since\nwe were little. We're always together.
MID_EV11_013_m01_030: Very nice to meet you both. Thank you for being\nsuch good friends to Cai.
MID_EV11_013_m01_051: What I don't understand is...how did Pop get married\nto a goddess?
MID_EV11_013_m01_052: Castor served in Aurora's temple as a vulkan.
MID_EV11_013_m01_053: And then one day, the two of them fled together.
MID_EV11_013_m01_054: What?!
MID_EV11_013_m01_055: I guess running off with a goddess is the type of thing\nthat gets you a death sentence.
MID_EV11_013_m01_031: Cai, may I have a better look at you?
MID_EV11_013_m01_032: You look so like me—your nose, your smile. But oh,\nI see Castor's kindness in your eyes.
MID_EV11_013_m01_033: Your being here, it's stirred my heart. I'm grateful\nyou made such a long journey to come and see me.
MID_EV11_013_m01_034: Oh! What's that in your left hand? Something's\nembedded in your palm!
MID_EV11_013_m01_035: Looks like a demon's eye. Guess it was hidden inside\nthat necklace you gave me when we parted ways.
MID_EV11_013_m01_036: The necklace? But how could this happen?
MID_EV11_013_m01_038: That jewel was originally given to me by my\nfather, Solel.
MID_EV11_013_m01_040: It seems different now. I can feel an ominous power\ncoming from it.
MID_EV11_013_m01_041: I'll try to find out what I can about it.
MID_EV11_013_m01_042: First, I'll scour the temple records.
MID_EV11_013_m01_043: I'll make my way to our lodgings in a while. I need to\ntalk to your mother a little more.
MID_EV11_013_m01_044: You head back. But when you want to see your mom\nagain, it's fine to come here later.
MID_EV11_013_m01_045: Centurio, think you can see these kids back to\nwhere we're staying?
MID_EV11_013_m01_046: Hey, and keep quiet about the secret shortcut, yeah?
MID_EV11_013_m01_047: Well, I mean, that's a serious breach of security.\nI can't just ignore it.
MID_EV11_013_m01_048: Listen to him, Centurio. If you tell anyone, I'll never\nforgive you.
MID_EV11_013_m01_049: Well! We should go. Wouldn't want to intrude on the\ngrown-ups' conversation.